00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:32.790
on how to build this financial model

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.410
on how to build this project.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.020
So without further ado,
let's start to jump into it.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.690
So let's start by talking about
what the SUMIFS function is

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:43.800
because as I told you, this
whole model will be strongly

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.910
built around the SUMIFS function.

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:48.830
And here we are on the official
Microsoft support page.

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:51.820
So this page tells us
that the SUMIFS function.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:55.900
One of the math and trig functions
adds all of its arguments

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.290
that meet multiple criteria.

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:02.090
So as the names says SUMIFS
you sum certain things,

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:05.260
if certain criterias are fulfilled.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.470
So how does the syntax of
this function look like?

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:11.010
And don't worry later we will explore

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:13.952
how this also looks on
a practical example.

00:02:13.952 --> 00:02:17.430
And the syntax demands to
first give the sum_range

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:18.970
to give a range of the numbers

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:20.780
that are supposed to be summed,

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.750
then to give a criteria_range.

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:26.000
So where the certain
things are to be found

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:27.770
that need to meet certain criteria,

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:30.670
then the criteria that
is supposed to be met.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:33.210
And then again, you
can add other criteria.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:34.850
So two, three, four, five,

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:37.010
pretty much how much criteria you want.

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:38.720
You can add them to the SUMIFS functions.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.260
So lets now look into
the practical example.

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:45.390
So let's imagine we have a
basic list of company data here.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:46.550
So here is your column

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.950
with the different customers
that you're serving.

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:50.490
Here we see a column of months,

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.070
and number of the sales.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:55.210
And let's just pretend this
is a transaction table.

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:58.260
So whenever a customer
makes a transaction,

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.390
then the month of the
transaction is recorded

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:01.850
and then the sales

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.350
then belongs to the
individual transaction.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:07.690
And now you can use SUMIFS formula

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:10.580
to add up based on certain criteria

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:13.250
to receive then the numbers by month.

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.260
And then also more
complicated version of that.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:17.240
But let's start by now understanding

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:19.730
what the sales per month are

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:21.760
that you find here in this table.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:24.630
And of course manually,
this would be pretty messy.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:27.380
You wouldn't want to
manually add all the numbers

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:31.300
in January, then all the
numbers and February and so on.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:34.040
But this is now where
SUMIFS formula can add.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:38.560
So here you start by typing
in this formula SUMIFS,

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.420
and then he wants the arguments.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.270
So these are the arguments
that we saw before.

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:45.590
So first the sum_range and
the range of the criteria,

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:47.700
and then the specific criteria.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.120
So let's start by the sum_range.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:50.823
So what is supposed to be summed up?

00:03:50.823 --> 00:03:54.400
Well this is the column of sales

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.870
and here it's good practice
to then also fix this.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.420
So by these dollar signs

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:01.780
and the windows hot key is F4

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:05.120
to directly get these fixed references.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:06.960
Now you're adding the criteria_range.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:10.420
So where is, Excel supposed to look at

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:11.730
to understand the criteria?

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:15.850
And here we only want to
sum up now the sales numbers

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:17.500
that belong to the individual months

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:18.890
as we will have them here in,

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:20.850
and which is why now we mark the

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:24.820
we highlight the month column
as the criteria column.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:27.750
And again, we make the fixed reference.

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:31.090
And now what is the specific
criteria that we wanna add?

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:32.210
And this is now the month

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:34.810
so we know click here on January

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:37.500
to fill the numbers for the first column.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:41.460
And now we see that apparently
in the month of January

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.070
of you know what it sum up

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:45.720
or the numbers we have here in January

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:49.090
this would sum up to the 33,000.

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:52.400
And because now we had all
the references correctly,

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:54.050
if we now fil it in,

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:56.830
we see all the numbers
for the specific months.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:00.230
So you see that now here
the columns they are fixed,

00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:01.980
but of course this moves.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:06.920
So here March, April,
May, June, July and so on.

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:09.510
And now if we sum all of this up.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:13.420
This gives us a total of 255,000.

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:14.950
And just as a little check.

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:18.440
So here just the basic, sums
formula that sums up numbers.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:20.230
We sum this up this way

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:22.470
this is exactly the sum
of all these figures,

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:23.520
which of course makes sense

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.450
because these are the only
months that are included.

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:27.580
So what do we need to do now

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:29.670
if we actually wanna have a full matrix

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:33.100
of not only the months,
but the customers as well?

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:34.900
So let's first understand

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:36.770
what all the distinct customers

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:38.150
are because as you can see,

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:40.237
many of them appear several times.

00:05:40.237 --> 00:05:43.490
And here, just the basic
little way to do that

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:47.640
is to use the remove duplicates function.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:50.399
So if you highlight
certain values like that,

00:05:50.399 --> 00:05:52.110
and then you press remove duplicates,

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:54.190
it's all selected correctly okay.

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:56.480
And then it takes out
all the duplicate values

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:59.130
and it only leaves with
the values that are unique.

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:01.360
So while there were 34 rows here

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:06.360
apparently only six distinct
customers were in this table.

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:09.170
Now by taking the list,
we can transpose it

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:11.370
because now we won't
have it in the row here.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.270
And by this, you mark this,

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:15.800
you copy it with control + C

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:18.130
and then you insert with paste special

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:19.500
and now here with transpose

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.980
to make sure that you get all these things

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:24.843
in this one line.

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:27.390
And now what we will use now

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:30.110
is a SUMIFS formula with two dimensions.

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:32.610
So with two different criterias.

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:34.560
So again, we use the SUMIFS.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.230
We start exactly the same way.

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:40.200
So the sum_range is the same column.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:44.530
And then we have the months
here first as the column,

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:46.980
and then as an additional value.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:50.510
But now, because won't actually
copy the formula all over,

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:54.030
make sure that you fix the column, right?

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:56.280
You do that by having the dollar sign

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:58.460
only in front of here,
the J the column symbol.

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:01.540
And if you press F4 several times,

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:03.640
you can target between
these different modes.

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:07.210
So now we have it set up
correctly for the first dimension,

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:09.820
but now we wanna do it for
the second dimension as well,

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:12.670
because now we do not only
want to have the criteria

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:16.010
that had only sums out the
values that have the right month,

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:19.310
but we also want to introduce
a second criteria on

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:22.230
Where the resemble supposed
to only sum up the values,

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:25.190
where it meets here the right customers.

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:28.730
So lets do that let's highlight
the customer's column again,

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:30.380
let's fix it.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:34.430
And now we need to
highlight this one here,

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:36.283
because you only want to calculate it

00:07:36.283 --> 00:07:38.000
if it has exactly the same values.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.657
So the same name from this column in here.

00:07:40.657 --> 00:07:41.490
And here again,

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:43.690
let's also make sure the
reference is correct,

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:46.380
but now we need to keep
the line, the row constant,

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:48.010
not the column, but the row,

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:49.580
which is why in all the dollar signs

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:54.580
is in front of the row number
instead of the column, letter.

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:57.950
And if we now close this.

00:07:57.950 --> 00:08:01.180
This apparently shows us
that the customer Paul

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:05.760
in the month of January
didn't do any revenues.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:09.520
But let's now look how it
may looks out with the rest.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:10.750
So if we now highlight this

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:13.260
and copy it over exactly in this way,

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:17.650
we see now that it really
fed off the exact values

00:08:17.650 --> 00:08:20.790
based on the name and the month

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:24.270
that they're most present
here in the table on the left.

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:27.490
And if again, now we make a sum

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:30.620
which pretty much sums up these values.

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:33.420
We would see that this is
of course the very same,

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:36.120
right, if we just add
together all these values.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:39.650
So this is the basic idea,
how SUMIFS function works,

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:41.000
how this function works.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.705
Again, you have first the sum_range

00:08:43.705 --> 00:08:46.060
for the value that you wanna sum up,

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:47.980
and then you can introduced pretty much

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:50.327
as many criteria as you want.

00:08:50.327 --> 00:08:52.710
So now I could even add
here a third criteria

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:54.630
and afterwards a forth and so on

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:56.640
to really take all of these
dimensions into account.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.870
So this, just to explain how SUMIFS works.

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:01.070
I hope this is clear

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:02.870
because now we're going to jump

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:04.250
into little financial models.

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:07.550
So what will we do now
with this SUMIFS function?

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:10.920
And my idea now was to build
a liquidity model with you.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:13.040
So it's just a simple little use case

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:15.030
out of financial modeling.

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:16.550
And the basic idea here

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:19.630
is that we want to forecast our liquidity.

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:23.980
So we want to forecast what
the cash of the company is,

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:26.610
and we wanna do that on a daily basis.

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:29.980
And so I just set up a very
simple little model here.

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:32.090
What you can see is that
here you have the dates.

00:09:32.090 --> 00:09:34.770
So this is now the year 2021.

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:37.900
So you can see it ranges
from 1st of January

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:39.650
until the very end

00:09:39.650 --> 00:09:43.730
it is going to be the
31st, the 31st of December.

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:45.780
So the last day of the year,

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:47.830
and then we have all the years in between,

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:49.240
since you have to count them here,

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:53.230
remember was as you have the 365 days

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:55.680
that we have here in the columns.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:58.630
And actually there is
a real story about this

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:00.910
because I once worked for a company,

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:02.500
it was one of our clients.

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:04.770
We had a liquidity model like this,

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:07.160
and of course it was a
bit more complicated.

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:09.550
This is obviously now
very simplified version

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:11.230
so with lots of more lines,

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:12.990
but pretty much a model like this.

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:15.610
Also every column, every day

00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:17.700
and what they were doing is

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:20.340
they weren't really
building this with formulas

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:22.877
as you can see currently
we are using right here

00:10:22.877 --> 00:10:24.540
and you can already see that

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:28.500
many of these things are
based on SUMIFS formulas,

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:32.080
but instead of that they
have a poor accountant

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:37.080
really filling in each
individual field by hand.

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:40.600
So there was one guy who
really put every single number

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:45.600
in every single field in this
huge spreadsheet of 365 days

00:10:45.950 --> 00:10:48.310
and then times all the
rows that he had here.

00:10:48.310 --> 00:10:51.730
And of course this was insane
in terms of the workload,

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:54.480
but then also in terms
of changing something.

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:57.000
So imagine how you do
some financial planning

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.090
and how your plan that
your personnel cost,

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:02.380
for instance later on down
the year increase or decrease

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:04.730
then you obviously look up
each individual sale by,

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:07.030
it was just insane.

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:09.560
But this is how people still work today

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:11.500
in many of these companies

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:13.230
and, you know, by taking this little,

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:15.110
by looking at this, little video,

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:16.620
I hope that you really understand

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:19.363
how you could do these
little things much easier.

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:22.650
And now I also put together here

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:24.450
just a simple little chart,

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:27.010
but you can pretty much see
how the liquidity evolves

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:29.900
over time based on this
simple little model

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:30.890
and what you see here.

00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:32.840
Now, this is, of course
also you can use it

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:34.060
from a business sense.

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:35.740
That by having a forecast like that,

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:38.500
you suddenly you realize
that you were okay in March,

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:40.100
I seem to be underwater, right?

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:42.090
My liquidity seems to be negative.

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:44.660
So probably need to be,
to revise my planning,

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:46.020
and to do something about

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:48.730
how I wanna handle my business operations,

00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:51.010
to make sure that somehow even this out,

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:52.740
to make sure that
actually I'm able to meet

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:54.650
all my financial obligations

00:11:54.650 --> 00:11:56.270
at this point in time,

00:11:56.270 --> 00:11:57.690
because of course, it looks like that

00:11:57.690 --> 00:11:59.900
in the end of the day
in the end of the year,

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:01.710
actually there is enough liquidity there

00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:04.320
just the temporary lack of liquidity

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:08.380
that someone needs to be,
of course taken care of.

00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:09.920
But that's now look into this model.

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:11.290
